# Frontend — Vue 3 islands

## When to use Vue vs Hotwire

| Vue                             | Hotwire                       |
| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Colour picker with live preview | Facet chip toggle, pagination |
| Room photo upload + results     | Add to cart, flash messages   |
| Multi-step interactive filter   | Lazy-loaded turbo frame       |

## Vue 3 conventions

- **No build step.** Vue is vendored at `vendor/javascript/vue.esm-browser.prod.js`
  and pinned in the importmap. That is the full browser build, so islands are
  plain `.js` modules exporting a component with a `template` string — SFCs
  would need a bundler, and one island does not justify one yet.
- Composition API (`setup()` with `ref`/`computed`) — not Options API
- Register the component in `app/javascript/islands/index.js`; the registry
  mounts anything carrying `data-island-component` and unmounts on
  `turbo:before-cache`
- Props from Rails as JSON in `data-island-props` on the mount element
- Fetch a **Rails** JSON endpoint (e.g. `/artworks/:slug/colour-matches`), not
  Hanami directly: colour ranking stays in the palette service, presentation
  (titles, prices, URLs, image variants) stays in Rails
- Handle loading, empty, and error states explicitly; guard against
  out-of-order responses
- Keep islands self-contained; no global Vue app wrapping the page

## Styling

- Tailwind utility classes in Vue templates
- Match server-rendered tile/card markup for consistency

## Avoid

- SPA router, Pinia store for app-wide state (not needed at this scale)
- Fetching catalogue data client-side when SSR already rendered it
